Aluminum alloys for low temperatures 
 
  
 
Aluminum and its alloys do not have a transition from ductile to brittle at low temperatures, moreover, their strength increases with decreasing temperature. The strength of aluminum alloys in stable states does not depend on the holding time at low temperatures. The same applies to strength at room temperature after holding at low temperature (Fig.. 1)[1]. 
 
The increase in the strength of aluminum alloys at low temperatures is insignificant to minus 50 ° C, but begins to increase significantly below minus 100 °С (rice. 1). 
 
The relative elongations of most aluminum alloys also increase as the temperature drops to minus 196 °C. For some alloys, especially with higher magnesium content (4.5% and above), elongation starts to decrease again, but not lower than the value for room temperature [1]. 
 
Shear strength, compression and collapse – everything rises at low temperatures. 
 
